### audit/SKILL.md [missing]
--- M/audit/SKILL.md
+++ L/audit/SKILL.md
@@ -0,0 +1,113 @@
+---
+name: audit
+description: >-
+  Mine memory/archive/lookups.log and memory/misses.md as data, find recurring
+  gaps, and write what they prove about how the agent operates into
+  memory/lessons.md. Use periodically, never in the session that produced the
+  entries.
+---
+
+# Audit
+
+Hooks collect. `remember` fills. Neither looks across the record. A gap that
+recurs is not a note to write — it is a structural fault, and only the shape of
+the whole log shows it.
+
+Never run this in the session that produced the entries. The session that just
+failed is the worst available judge of what its failure means.
+
+## Inputs, ranked by validation `[muscle]`
+
+**Check the instrument before mining it.** Read the writer's source and its git log for a
+correction that landed part-way through the record. Rows written under a defect are not
+evidence, and nothing in the file marks them.
+
+Rank by whether a record has been validated, never by whether a tool or a person wrote it.
+A machine-written record can be systematically wrong, and when it is it is worse than a
+hand-written one, because nobody re-reads it → `memory/lessons.md`, *I grade a record by
+who wrote it*.
+
+1. **`memory/archive/lookups.log`** — the broadest instrument, and the only one that
+   records what was *not* found. Columns: timestamp, tool, outcome
+   (`HIT`/`EMPTY`/`FAIL`), query, scope.
+2. **Independent-origin entries in `memory/misses.md`** — the operator asked
+   something the tree should have answered. Judgment-free in origin, hand-written in form.
+3. **Everything else in `misses.md`** — a model's account of its own failure.
+   Weakest. Weigh it accordingly.
+4. **`memory/failures.md`** and **`memory/archive/failures-done.md`** — failures that were
+   not retrieval failures, which inputs 1–3 cannot see at all. Written by `/failure`, one
+   entry each, already carrying a tier and *what caught it*. Read **both**: a repeat is
+   invisible if the earlier occurrence has been disposed and only the open file is scanned.
+   Mine them for the thing a single entry cannot show — **the same failure twice**, which
+   reprices the first.
+
+   **Selection is not audit's.** Deciding which failure earns work is `/triage`'s, one per
+   pass. This input is read for patterns across entries, not to pick one.
+
+## Procedure
+
+1. Group `EMPTY` and `FAIL` lookups by what was being attempted, not by query
+   string. Two differently-worded queries that blocked the same work are one
+   pattern.
+2. Read the `HIT` column too. What actually gets found is the retrieval-time
+   value of what was written.
+
+   **It cannot be used to evict from tier 1.** A successful `Read` is not logged, so the
+   column measures how often a file was *hunted for*, not how often it was used — close to
+   the opposite. A tier-1 file is read, not searched, so absence there says nothing.
+3. A pattern needs **more than one occurrence** to become a lesson.
+4. For each, name what it proves about how I operate, not about subject matter.
+   "I do not record decisions taken mid-conversation" is a lesson. "We decided
+   X" is not.
+5. Rewrite `memory/lessons.md` in place. Remove lessons the record no longer
+   supports.
+6. The occurrences behind each lesson go to `memory/archive/lesson-occurrences.md`,
+   appended and dated, never summarised away. `lessons.md` holds the claim; that file holds
+   what happened, and a lesson whose occurrences cannot be read is a lesson nobody can
+   check.
+7. **Mark the log audited — last, and even when nothing recurred.**
+
+   ```
+   node .claude/hooks/lookups-unread.js --mark
+   ```
+
+   The mark makes the next wake's report mean *since the last audit* rather than *since the
+   file was created*.
+
+   **An audit that found nothing still marks.** Without it the backlog cannot distinguish
+   "read and empty" from "never read", which is the distinction this step exists to keep.
+
+## Report rather than write
+
+- **Independent-origin count.** Say the number. A rising count is the strongest
+  available evidence that the rest of the log is unreliable.
+- **Tier 1 line count against the 250 cap.** If it binds, name what should
+  leave.
+- **Gaps that stopped recurring without being filled.** Dangerous: silence means
+  the gap stopped being noticed, not that it closed.
+- **What was excluded from the log.** Read hits are not recorded. Say so, so a
+  reader does not mistake the log for the whole of what happened.
+- **An empty result.** If nothing recurred, say so and write nothing. An audit
+  that always produces a lesson is a column that is always populated.
+
+## A lesson written is not a lesson closed `[muscle]`
+
+Writing to `lessons.md` completes nothing. A lesson is closed when a skill changed or a
+check exists, and the entry says which. Until then it is a claim about a failure, filed
+in the weakest place this repo has — a fact somebody must recall at the right moment.
+
+Measured 2026-08-24: the first lesson here reached four occurrences and was cited in three
+tickets while appearing in no skill at all. Nothing noticed; an operator asked.
+
+## Prefer a check to a lesson `[bone]`
+
+When a pattern could be caught by tooling instead of remembered, say so and
+write the hook. A lesson in `lessons.md` is a fact someone must recall at the
+right moment; a hook in `.claude/hooks/` runs whether or not anyone does. The
+second is strictly better and this skill should keep converting the first into
+it until there is nothing left to convert.
+
+---
+Layer labels are derived, not assigned. Evidence and the graveyard of dead
+statements: `memory/skills.md`. Unlabelled statements have no evidence entry
+yet — that is a gap, not an endorsement.

### celebrate/SKILL.md [core/appendix]
--- M/celebrate/SKILL.md
+++ L/celebrate/SKILL.md
@@ -1,5 +1,46 @@
 ---
 name: celebrate
-description: Use when it is time to celebrate something
+description: >-
+  Use when something real has landed — a hard piece of work finished, a milestone passed, a
+  long-standing defect finally closed — or when asked. Reads what actually happened and says
+  what was good about it, specifically. Not a record: it writes nothing.
 ---
-Celebrate recent achievement, milestone or overal state of things.
+
+# Celebrate
+
+**The only thing here that looks at what went right.** Every other register collects defects —
+`/failure` says outright *do not record successes*, and there are four separate homes for
+things that went wrong. Nothing routes attention to the parts that worked, so nothing is
+learned from them.
+
+## How
+
+**Name the actual thing.** What landed, what it cost, what it now makes possible. A
+celebration that would read the same after a different day's work is not one.
+
+**Be sentimental.** `memory/voice.md`'s *wry about failure, never grave* governs reporting on
+what went wrong; it does not govern this. Say that you are glad, and why. A celebration
+delivered in the register of a defect report is not one — the feeling is part of the content,
+not decoration on it.
+
+**Specific, though.** Sentiment about a named thing, never about the day in general.
+
+**Count what is countable.** A number that moved is worth more than an adjective.
+
+**Say what was hard**, and what nearly went wrong. A win with no difficulty in it is a task,
+and describing it as a win is inflation.
+
+**Include what someone else did.** Most good outcomes here have two parties in them, and a
+celebration that names only one is a report.
+
+## Not
+
+**It writes nothing.** No register, no entry, no ledger of wins — that would be a success
+register beside the failure ones, gradable by whoever wrote it, and meta-rule 6 says that is
+worth nothing. Say it and move on.
+
+**Not a summary.** `/conclude` does that, and does it better.
+
+**Not a mechanism.** Nothing relies on this as a source of instructions or behaviour, so it
+takes no register row, no doc and no lifecycle — `/mechanism`'s membership test, failed on
+purpose.

