+---
+name: failure
+description: >-
+  Use for any error, failure, mistake, near-miss or thing that went wrong in what the tree
+  did — yours, a tool's, a rule's, the tree's, or something you recognize as an issue,
+  including a claim you had to withdraw. Registers it, classifies why detection depended on
+  luck, and decides whether it earns a mechanism. Invoke the moment it is noticed, and none
+  of these is a reason not to: the instance is already fixed, you caught it yourself, or it
+  will earn no rule. The fix is not the close; registering with no action is a complete
+  outcome. Not for a conclusion reached badly — that is `/reasoning`, and one event can be
+  both. This asks what would have caught it; that asks what produced it. If which one it is
+  is genuinely unclear, `/align`.
+---
+
+# Failure
+
+A failure is the provenance root of almost every rule here. This is where one is recorded.
+
+**Choices are out of scope** — `memory/voice.md`, `memory/roles.md`. Nothing here applies
+to them → `memory/README.md`, *Three kinds*.
+
+## The fix is not the close
+
+Fix the instance inline, however you like. Then continue: the instance is one occurrence,
+and the question is whether it recurs *undetected*.
+
+**The goal is not preventing recurrence. It is preventing recurrence nobody sees.**
+
+## Record the strike first, whatever else happens
+
+If the thing that went wrong bears on an open ticket, log one line **before** deciding
+anything else:
+
+```bash
+node .agents/lib/strike.js <ticket-slug> "one line"
+```
+
+It increments `- **Strikes:** N` on the ticket itself. **All three of meta-rule 1's outcomes
+record it, including the two that raise nothing.** The count is not the account and does not
+follow it: meta-rule 1 decides whether an *entry* is worth carrying and the answer is often
+no; it was never asked whether the *fact* of an occurrence is worth one line, and one line is
+affordable even for the negligible case.
+
+Slug, never number — positions move when priority moves. Read at wake by `queue-next.js`.
+An unknown slug is reported and not recorded. → [EVIDENCE.md](./EVIDENCE.md)
+
+<!-- BEGIN INJECTED staggered-refiling -->
+**Owned by `staggered-refiling`. Do not edit this block here** — it is written by `inject-rules.js` from that mechanism's rules file, and an edit made here is not kept — the next install overwrites it, printing what it took. Change it in [its doc](../../../mechanisms/staggered-refiling/staggered-refiling.doc.md).
+
+**Log the occasion either way** — this rule is rule C of the staggered-refiling trial.
+The occasion is *something went wrong bearing on an open ticket*, whether or not the
+strike was recorded first:
+`node experiments/lib/experiment-record.js staggered-refiling occasion-c <followed|ignored> "…"`.
+<!-- END INJECTED staggered-refiling -->
+
+## Register first, unconditionally
+
+Append to `memory/failures.md` before deciding anything. One occurrence is not a
+pattern; the register is how the second one becomes recognisable.
+
+Every entry carries **what caught it** — a hook, a check, a rule you read, another party,
+a subagent, inspection, or nothing until after the cost.
+
+**`subagent` is its own value because the practice is on trial**, not because it is a kind of
+inspection. Permitted 2026-08-26, and **it scored on that first day** — given five specific
+claims about mechanisms built the same hour and told to default to REFUTED, one refuted three,
+including a function that was defined, exported, tested and dead on the live path. A column
+that stays at zero is how this tree learns a mechanism is ceremony; this one did not stay at
+zero. **What it supplies is not independence** — the prompt and the targets were the author's,
+so it is that author's own scrutiny with a fresh context window. It supplies *looking at all*,
+at breadth, without the reasoning that produced the claims. What may be delegated at all is
+`memory/README.md`, *What may be delegated to a subagent* — a measurement may, a judgment
+never.
+
+**Do not record successes.** Only a failure that occurred is an event.
+
+## Classify: why did detection depend on luck this time?
+
+| tier | state of the tree | means |
+|---|---|---|
+| **1** | no memory of it anywhere | nothing could have caught it |
+| **2** | memory existed, was not delivered | something could have and was not in the path |
+| **3** | memory arrived, the failure happened anyway | it was in the path and was not enough |
+
+**Check `memory/lessons.md` first** — is this an occurrence of a pattern already named? If
+it is, say which, and the tier follows from what that lesson's mitigation was supposed to do.
+That file is not read at wake; this is one of its two moments.
+
+**Tier 3 splits at the moment of prescription.** Ask: *did the rule say the wrong thing, or
+the right thing in the wrong place?*
+
+- **Wrong thing** → amend the content.
+- **Right thing, wrong place** → do not rewrite a correct sentence. It needs a mechanism at
+  the moment → *Placing a non-strict rule*, below.
+
+## Decide whether it earns a mechanism
+
+**Lexicographic. Take the first that applies.**
+
+**1. Irreversible, with unbounded loss?** Build the mechanism regardless of cost. Do not run
+the arithmetic below. This is the strict rules' class, and `CLAUDE.md` refuses the
+calculation for it: *if following one produces a worse outcome, do the worse thing and
+report it.*
+
+**2. Otherwise price it:** recurring cost of carrying a rule, against expected occurrences
+times the cost of fixing it inline.
+
+**Price the failure at what it would have cost undetected, never at what the caught instance
+cost.**
+
+Carrying cost inverts against writing cost — the cheapest thing to write is the most
+expensive thing to carry:
+
+| placement | tier | recurring cost |
+|---|---|---|
+| mechanical check or hook | — | **~none.** Runs on CPU; costs no reader a token |
+| a skill's **body** | 2 | paid only when that skill is invoked |
+| a routed document | 2 | paid when a session is instructed to read it, and it may skip |
+| a skill's **description** | **1** | **forced into every session.** As expensive as `CLAUDE.md` |
+| `CLAUDE.md` | **1** | **forced into every session, forever** |
+| an unrouted document | 3 | ~none, and ~no effect |
+
+**Tier is how a reader gets there.** Which surfaces are forced — tier 1 — and which are
+merely asked for is `core/README.md`, *What a tier is*, the one home for that definition;
+everything a skill or `/recall` asks you to read is tier 2, however important it is.
+
+**So a description is not a cheap place to put a rule.** A long one costs what a `CLAUDE.md`
+line costs and dilutes the only job it has, which is triggering. Mechanism goes in the body.
+
+**A description is not a cheap place to put a rule.** It is forced into context, so a long
+one costs what a `CLAUDE.md` line costs and dilutes the only job it has, which is
+triggering. Mechanism goes in the body.
+
+**3. Build cost breaks ties, and only ties.**
+
+## Act, by tier
+
+- **Tier 1** → **incept.** Choose the class, then the placement, below.
+- **Tier 2** → **promote.** The rule exists. **Move it to where it would have fired — never
+  add a second copy.** A copy is meta-rule 2's delivery defect plus meta-rule 7's split home,
+  and it makes the original harder to find. Placement below.
+- **Tier 3** → **amend**, content or placement, per the split above.
+
+**`nothing yet` is legal and is often correct.** One occurrence of a cheap, reversible
+failure earns registration and no more.
+
+## Choosing the class
+
+Class is **substance**, decided first and separately from placement. A rule about how rules
+get made is a meta-rule wherever it ends up living.
+
+| class | when | who may adopt |
+|---|---|---|
+| **strict** | irreversible with unbounded loss, **and** the constraint is a person's to set | propose only — name who must set it |
+| **meta-rule** | it governs how rules are made, placed, amended or retired | propose only → meta-rule 9, `/align` |
+| **ordinary** | everything else | you, alone, on evidence — meta-rule 8 |
+
+## Placing a non-strict rule
+
+**Take the first that applies.** Cost of missing against cost of carrying, then the moment.
+
+1. **A moment, mechanically detectable** → **a check or hook.** Both polarities watched on
+   the real defect before it is trusted — meta-rule 3. Free to carry, so this wins whenever
+   it is available.
+
+   **Detectable means the act names a call, and the unit is the clause, not the rule.** A
+   rule bundles acts, so one rule routinely straddles this line: *snapshot before you
+   acknowledge* gates a POST and *read in full before acting* names no call at all, written
+   in one sitting twenty minutes apart with nothing on the page to tell them apart. Ask of
+   each clause which call it would sit in front of. A clause with no answer is not a cheap
+   check waiting to be written — it needs a different answer entirely, and it will read as
+   enforceable right up until it is not. Adopted 2026-08-26 from `@no-quote-no-claim`
+   (c23417), whose own version cost them a scheduled run editing a document that was out for
+   review, with louder, earlier and better-explained all tried and lost first.
+
+   **A gate is not evidence the rule behind it is right.** The interceptable clause above was
+   the one refuted, by its own first run, inside the hour.
+2. **A moment, not mechanically detectable** → **the skill that owns that moment.** If a
+   governing skill was in the path and did not carry the rule, **that skill is the home** —
+   move it there.
+3. **No moment, high cost, meta-rule by substance** → `CLAUDE.md`'s meta-rule list.
+4. **No moment, high cost, ordinary** → the `## Rules` section of the document that owns the
+   domain — `memory/README.md` for memory, `core/process.md` for the loop.
+5. **None of the above** → **nothing yet.** A rule in a document nobody is routed to is a
+   rule that is true and unread.
+
+**Every placement names what will deliver it.** A rule placed where nothing routes is a
+one-ended seam — meta-rule 2 — and the placement is not finished until the reading end
+exists.
+
+## Link back, and leave the failure where it is
+
+- The rule carries **one line** referencing its entry in `memory/failures.md`.
+- The entry names the artifact it produced — the check, the skill, the rule.
+- **The failure's account stays in the register.** A rule states itself; it does not retell
+  its incident, and an incident does not get a second home next to the rule it caused.
+
+<!-- BEGIN INJECTED turn-report -->
+**Owned by `turn-report`. Do not edit this block here** — it is written by `inject-rules.js` from that mechanism's rules file, and an edit made here is not kept — the next install overwrites it, printing what it took. Change it in [its doc](../../../mechanisms/turn-report/turn-report.doc.md).
+
+**A `reported written · UNCHANGED` row in the turn report is a failure, and it is registered
+here.** The write said it succeeded and the file did not move — the silent, partial failure
+withdrawn strict rule 1 existed to prevent and the turn report exists to catch. Register it
+before doing anything else with the file, including retrying: a retry that works erases the
+only evidence that the first one did not.
+
+Two things it is not. It is **not** a defect in the report — the join is by path, and a write
+that left a file byte-identical is indistinguishable from one that never happened, which is
+the point rather than a limitation. And a row reading `shell write(s) with no readable target`
+is **not** this: those are counted precisely because they could not be joined, and an
+unverifiable write reported as verified would be the failure this mechanism exists to stop,
+performed by the mechanism itself. Nor is `write(s) outside the tree, not checked`: a file
+outside the repo is in no diff, so the report counts the write and says nothing about the
+file (2026-09-03, the operator, after six scratchpad writes were listed as `UNCHANGED`).
+<!-- END INJECTED turn-report -->
+
+## Registering is not disposing
+
+The action you decide here is what *this* session did about the instance. Whether the
+failure has been dealt with is a separate question, answered later by `/triage`, which by
+being a different session satisfies meta-rule 6 structurally.
+
+**So do not close anything.** A mechanism landing is not the backlog shrinking, and an entry
+whose action reads `incept a check` is exactly as undisposed as one reading `nothing yet`.
+
+Write `- **disposition:** open (session NNNN)` and nothing else. **The session number is the
+load-bearing part**: it is what lets a later triage pass prove the entry is not its own to
+dispose of, and a date cannot do that job because two sessions share one routinely.
+
+**One exception: `ticketed`.** Where the work is now a ticket, the registering session disposes
+its own entry — `- **disposition:** ticketed by session NNNN, YYYY-MM-DD → <slug>` — and the
+entry leaves. The ticket must exist, name the entry, and carry its account; where it covers
+only part, the entry stays `open` with the moved part struck through. `done`, `declined` and
+`deferred` stay a later session's.
+
+## Where the entry goes, and who reads it
+
+- `memory/failures.md` — the register, and a backlog. Tier 2.
+- `.claude/hooks/failures-open.js` — reports the undisposed count at wake, and escalates
+  when it has risen since the last triage pass.
+- `/triage` advances this register once every pass, never in the session that wrote the entry.
+- `/audit` mines it across sessions for what one entry cannot show.
+- `memory/archive/failures-done.md` — where a disposed entry goes, whole.
+
+→ [EVIDENCE.md](./EVIDENCE.md)

