+# maintain — evidence
+
+Why this skill exists and what each statement was written against. The mechanism it serves is
+M24, `mechanisms/maintenance/`; its resolutions are
+[0059](../../../docs/tickets/0059-a-mechanism-is-rechecked-when-what-governs-it-moves.md).
+
+## Why a skill at all, when the rules were already injected
+
+**Because the injected version had no middle.** Until 2026-08-30 the whole instruction for
+maintaining a mechanism was one sentence inside `/mechanism`'s Amend step: *re-check the due
+level against what governs it, and move the mark.* A trigger and a closing act, and nothing
+between them.
+
+**Measured on the session that found it.** Two maintenances that day — M06 and M24 — and the
+first produced a doc section describing what was wrong, moved the mark, and stopped. That
+satisfied every word of the rule: it re-checked, it recorded, it marked. The operator's
+correction was *"this was supposed to be the maintain process, action, not documenting what is
+wrong."*
+
+**The reasoning that produced the null act is worth keeping**, because it was not laziness
+alone. The pass argued that writing the missing script would be *building a ranked ticket
+without grading it* — meta-rule 6 applied to an act it does not govern. That rule is about who
+may tick a box. It says nothing about who may write code, and sessions build ticketed work
+constantly. → `memory/reasoning.md`, 2026-08-30.
+
+## The cascade, and why *move both* is a rule rather than advice
+
+The two mark levels were built as a pair and read as independent. The same session moved
+`rules` on M06 and on M24 and left `output` due on both — legal under *re-check the due level*,
+singular.
+
+**What that leaves is worse than an unfinished pass.** The records stay validated against a
+rule that no longer exists, and the wake then reports `output` due with the line *records
+changed in N sessions* — a true statement about churn and a false account of the cause. The
+trigger was upstream and nothing recovers it.
+
+## Author-distance, and why it was dropped
+
+It came from `/audit`'s reasoning — the main session wrote many of the entries it would be
+re-checking — and it was correct while a records maintenance meant a session **reading** the
+records. 0059 resolution 8 changed the act: `format never content, live rows only`, performed
+by a `<slug>.maintain.js`.
+
+**A format check contains no judgment, so there is no bias for distance to protect against**,
+and a script's output is identical whoever runs it. Kept as a condition for the residual case —
+a record that genuinely cannot be scripted — rather than as a default that no longer describes
+the work.
+
+## Why the five steps are ordered, and why `/denoise` is inside the order
+
+**Build-up is first because a missing part makes every step below it unanswerable rather than
+passing.** A doc that does not exist cannot be compared against `/mechanism`, and the comparison
+returning nothing looks identical to the comparison finding nothing wrong.
+
+**Denoise follows each amend rather than sitting at the end, because amending only ever adds.**
+A doc that has just gained a paragraph is precisely when the superseded one is still beside it.
+And step 4 *reads that doc* to decide what the records must satisfy — a doc that says a thing
+twice makes the records check ambiguous, so denoising before it is correctness rather than
+tidiness.
+
+**It also supplies the occasion `/denoise` has never had.** `/triage`'s sweep names the gap in
