+# Evidence — ticket
+
+Why this skill says what it says, and what would retire each statement. The instruction is
+in `SKILL.md`; nothing here is repeated there.
+
+## Quiz only what is genuinely forked
+
+**The statement:** register an uncontroversial decomposition and report it, attended or not.
+
+**What it replaced, 2026-08-26.** The step read *where a decomposition is uncontroversial and
+the session is unattended, register it and report rather than blocking.* It keyed the escape
+hatch on **unattended**, which is a proxy for *nobody can answer* — and the proxy inverts the
+moment someone is present. An attended session with an uncontroversial breakdown was
+instructed to block.
+
+**The specimen, one occurrence, and the evidence is thin — meta-rule 8.** Session 0016 split a
+guard-coverage failure into an AFK ticket and a HITL ticket, presented three options, marked
+one *(Recommended)*, and asked. The operator took the recommendation and said: *if it is safe
+to do, then why ask?*
+
+**The tell that generalises:** having a recommendation you expect to be taken. A genuine fork
+is one where you cannot say which branch you would pick; anything else is a question shaped
+like a decision. Availability is not a licence to spend attention.
+
+**Not adopted as a meta-rule.** It governs one skill's step, not how rules get made, so it is
+ordinary and adopted alone on evidence. The wider class — every moment the loop hands control
+to a human — is [0036](../../../docs/tickets/0036-escalation-register.md) and is `/align`'s.
+
+**What would retire this statement:** an uncontroversial decomposition registered without a
+quiz that the operator would have redirected. One is enough; the rule was adopted on one.
+
+## `Grading — the act`, and where it lived before
+
+**Written 2026-08-31, on two askings by the operator one day apart** — *"how do I grade 31 and
+67?"* on the 30th, *"also give me instructions to grade 67 and 31"* on the 31st. Same pair,
+unprompted both times, from the one human reader this repo has.
+
+**The tree had a worklist, a field, an enforcement rule and no procedure.** `core/process.md`
+stage 0 says *check what the last session claimed* and marks itself *(no skill owns this
+stage)*. This appendix's `Grading` row said who may write the field and when. `core/README.md`
+says grading a criterion is a judgment and stopped there. There was no `/grade`.
+
+**The one real instruction was a string inside a script's output** — `grading-due.js` printing
+*Read each criterion against the artifact. This list is not a verdict.* That is `/mechanism`'s
+**`embedded`** exactly: covered in practice, wrong home, reachable only by someone who had
+already run the script. It now points here instead of restating (meta-rule 7).
+
+**The first answer went into chat, and that is why there was a second asking.** The miss logged
+on the 30th diagnosed the wrong-home problem correctly and then put its own replacement
+somewhere worse. `memory/misses.md` set the bar in its own words — *first occurrence is a miss,
+a second is grounds* — and the second arrived within a day.
+
+**Why an appendix section and not a skill.** The act has a moment `/ticket`'s description
+already names — closing — and the rule is project-local throughout: this repo's `Grading`
+field, `grading-due.js`, `close-ticket.js`. A skill would be a second home for a field
+documented here.
+
+**Where *running the commands is not the grading* comes from.** `memory/reasoning.md`
+2026-08-30, `property-substituted`: three rounds of runnable commands were handed to the
+operator as *don't trust my tests*, all of which had already been run with their output
+correctly predicted. Meta-rule 6 protects the claim, not the keystroke.
+
+**What would retire the section:** a `/grade` skill, if the act ever grows a procedure long
+enough to need its own trigger. Then this shortens to a pointer rather than being deleted.
+
+**2026-09-01 — the *closing is the moment* reasoning above met its counter-example, and the
+description now names grading itself.** A session finished a live board round holding fresh
+grading evidence for 0017's four built criteria — the exact non-builder-with-artifacts moment
+— and stopped at *offering* a grading pass, because nothing in forced context claimed the act:
+*close one whose criteria have been graded* does not fire for a session whose criteria are not
+yet graded. The operator had to ask. Two other layers failed the same day and are the failure
+register's (`memory/failures.md` 2026-09-01, the announcer's prefix test) — but the trigger
