+
+# Triage
+
+One pass picks **one** item and disposes of it. Picking is a separate act from doing, and
+separating them is the point: a backlog nobody selects from is worked by whoever is standing
+there when it binds, which is selection by accident.
+
+**Selection is not execution, and a pass names both.** Triage names the item, its
+disposition, and **the skill that will resolve it** — `/implement` for work to be built,
+`/ticket` to mint or close, `/comment` to reply. **A pick with a rank and no named owner is a
+backlog entry that has been sorted**, which is the state the pass exists to leave behind.
+
+**What kind of build it is, is not this pass's question.** `/implement` asks it first and
+routes on. It also carries the verification a pass does not have — what must be read before
+the code, which checks are watched failing, and who may not grade the result — so a pass that
+sorts the owners itself hands the work on without any of that.
+
+This bounds the pass; it does not forbid finishing something small. Where the work is minutes
+and the item is the ruin class — irreversible, unbounded loss — do it now and dispose `done`.
+Routing a ruin-class item through a ticket to observe a separation of duties delays the one
+class `/failure` says takes a mechanism regardless of cost. **The test is whether the pass
+stays bounded**, not whether any work happened inside it.
+
+## Every backlog is advanced, one item each
+
+**A pass advances every backlog, and takes one item from each.** Not one item for the pass:
+one per backlog, so none of them waits for its turn. A backlog reached only every third pass
+is a backlog whose selection is days old while reading as current.
+
+**Advancing means something different in each, and the pass owes the one its backlog takes** —
+a ranking where nothing leaves, a row disposed where rows leave, a mark moved where a mark is
+what goes quiet. Deciding it is "dispose one from each" is how the pass becomes the thing the
+old quota of one was guarding against.
+
+**Where a backlog cannot be advanced, the pass says so and why.** Empty, or everything in it
+written by this session, or its only eligible row blocked on someone else — each is a complete
+outcome and each is *reported*, never left as a gap a reader has to notice. **A skipped
+backlog and an unmentioned one look identical**, which is the whole reason the sentence is
+here.
+
+**One item per backlog, never two.** The size objection the old quota existed for is
+unrefuted: a pass that grows is a pass that gets skipped, and nothing has measured what size
+that begins at.
+
+**If a backlog grows across passes, that is the finding, not a reason to raise the quota
+quietly.** Report it. Either the quota moves, by whoever set it, or the bar for entering the
+backlog is too loose — and which of those it is, is worth knowing.
+
+*(the operator, 2026-09-02, moving the quota they set on 2026-08-26 → [0062](../../../docs/tickets/0062-disposal-rate-is-somebody-s-number.md).
+It buys coverage rather than convergence, and that ticket carries the arithmetic saying so.)*
+
+## Never dispose of what you wrote
+
+An item registered by this session may be picked by a later one, never by this one. The
+constraint is meta-rule 6 and it is satisfied by *who is looking*, not by looking carefully.
+
+Where the backlog records a date and a session, the filter is mechanical. Apply it before
+ranking, so an ineligible item never competes.
+
+**A pass may not elect its own rows.** A ticket this session minted does not take the front,
+however well it would rank; say so where it would have.
+
+**A writer may dispose its own entry as `ticketed`** — conditions in `/failure`,
+*Registering is not disposing*.
+
+## Order within a backlog, never across
+
+**There is no ordering across backlogs, because every one of them is advanced.** What the
+loop's stage order still decides is where the pass's attention sits when a backlog offers two
+comparable rows.
+
+Rank the items *within* each backlog by **cost** — what recurrence or delay would cost,
+against what acting costs. Never by age: oldest-first is the absence of a decision, and it
+reliably surfaces the cheapest item rather than the one that matters.
+
+**Rank against the tree, not against the row.** A backlog entry is a dated claim, and the
+thing it describes may have been repaired, renamed or overtaken since. Re-read what the
+top candidates actually assert before ranking them; a strike count and an entry's own account
+are evidence of what was true when written and of nothing later.
+
+The cost test itself is `/failure`'s and is not repeated here.
+
+## Dispositions
+
+Every pick ends in exactly one, and each names something checkable. **An item with no
+disposition stays in the backlog** — absence is not clearance.
+
+- **Ticketed** — the work outlives this session. Mint through `/ticket`; the ticket's
+  criteria now own it. The commonest outcome for anything that needs building.
+- **Declined** — priced and refused, with the price written down. **A decline with no price
+  is a deferral wearing better clothes.**
+- **Deferred** — kept, with the condition that brings it back stated. Never a bare *later*.
+  A deferral whose condition cannot be recognised when it arrives is a decline that has not
+  admitted it.
+- **Done** — the work landed and nothing residual remains. It claims no more than that.
+- **Ordered** — the pass ranked the backlog and removed nothing from it. The checkable thing
+  is the ranking: which item is next and what it beat, with the price of the move written
+  down. *(the operator, 2026-08-29)*
+
+Deferred items stay in the backlog. **Ordered leaves the whole backlog in place** — it is the
+only disposition that is about the list rather than about one item on it. The other three
+leave it, whole, for wherever that backlog archives.
+
+**Ordered is the ticket queue's ordinary outcome and the others' rare one.** On failures and
+concerns the label *is* the exit: the item is disposed of and leaves in the same act. A pass
+over the queue removes nothing — the tickets stay until their boxes are checked, which is
+`/ticket`' act and not a pass's — so without this word a queue pass had nothing to write
+and left no trace even on the days it happened. *Declined* and *Deferred* still apply to a
+single ticket and are unchanged. → [0054](../../../docs/tickets/done/0054-a-queue-pass-leaves-a-record.md).
+
+## The inward sweep
+
+Every backlog above is fed by something going wrong. Nothing routes attention to the parts
+of the tree that work — so a mechanism that has been superseded, duplicated, or outgrown its
+reason is never looked at again, because working correctly produces no entry anywhere.
+
+**Occasionally a pass sweeps instead of selecting.** One region of the standing corpus —
+mechanism, not prose — read for what could be **generalised, simplified, or dropped without
+losing anything**. Rotate the region; record where the sweep landed so the next starts
+elsewhere. Reading the same region repeatedly produces a corpus that is tidy in one corner.
+
+**A sweep is a census, never a framework.** Report unevenness with its specimens. An
+abstraction proposed over a family hides an instance's defect better than it prevents it,
+and a corpus of specific failures is not improved by a general answer to them.
+
+### The bar, which is what stops this being taste
+
+Every finding names its **specimens**, what it **costs now**, and **what would break**. A
+finding that cannot say what removing it risks has not been understood well enough to file.
+
+- **Generalise** needs **two or more existing instances**, both named. One instance and a
+  prediction is a framework.
+- **Simplify** names what would be deleted and the check that would still pass afterwards.
+- **Drop** needs the **purpose** to be gone or subsumed, and names what subsumes it.
+  **Quiet is not evidence of useless** — a guard on a rare irreversible act is silent by
+  design, and low activity is the signature of the mechanisms that must never be removed.
+- **Duplicate** covers verbatim and near-verbatim restatement as well as duplicated logic —
+  one canonical home per fact, everywhere else a pointer.
+- **Compact** merges claims that say the same thing and archives the pre-merge text, so the
+  standing version shortens without losing a distinction. **Reversibility is the condition**,
+  not a nicety: a merge nobody can unmake is a deletion that has not admitted it.
+
+**Prose is in scope and its procedure is not written here.** A sweeping pass covers both
+substrates, and where the finding is prose it **invokes `/denoise`** rather than restating
+its rules — dedup, the pointer form, and the evolution-trim are that skill's and stay there.
+The sweep supplies the occasion, which is what prose reduction has never had: `/denoise`
+runs on request and nothing has ever created the request.
+
+**A sweep proposes and never cuts.** Findings leave as tickets through `/ticket`, so the
+session that spotted something is not the session that grades its removal — meta-rule 6, and
+here it is the whole safeguard, because removal is the one disposition that cannot be undone
+by noticing later.
+
+**A sweep that finds nothing is a result.** Record the region and move on. A pass that
+manufactures a finding to justify itself has inverted the bar.
+
+## Close the pass
+
+Per backlog: what was picked, its disposition, its resolving skill, and the size of that
+backlog when you finished — **including the backlogs that could not be advanced, with the
+reason**. The size is what makes the next pass able to see growth; without it a backlog has no
+memory of its own trend and every pass reads as the first.
+
+**One pass, one number.** Every marker the pass writes carries the same pass number, so the
+three registers stay joinable and a reader can tell one sweep across all of them from three
+passes that happened to fall on one day.
+
+**Then hand on, in the same session.** A pick with a named owner is invoked, not recorded: a
+picked ticket goes to `/implement`, which routes on; a picked dueness row goes to `/maintain`;
+a picked inbox item goes to `/comment`. The record says what the hand-on did. **The one pass
+that closes on a pick with no work is one whose instruction was selection only**, and the record
+says so in those words. *(the operator, 2026-09-03)*
